            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "POLYNOMIALS"
            }
          ],
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.376,
            0.28,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.1 Introduction"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.414,
            0.901,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Class IX, you have studied polynomials in one variable and their degrees. Recall that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\mathrm { i f } }   p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial in x, the highest power of x in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( \\vec { x } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the degree of the polynomial",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4x + 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial in the variable x of degree "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 , 2 y ^ { 2 } - 3 y + 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial in the variable y of degree 2, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5x^{3}-4x^{2}+x-\\sqrt{2}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial in the variable x of degree 3 and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "7u^{6}-\\frac{3}{2}u^{4}+4u^{2}+u-8"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial in the variable u of degree 6. Expressions like "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{1}{x - 1}, \\sqrt{x} + 2, \\frac{1}{x^2 + 2x + 3}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " etc., are not polynomials."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.622,
            0.899,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A polynomial of degree 1 is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear polynomial",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". For example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 x - 3 ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sqrt{3}x+5, y+\\sqrt{2}, x-\\frac{2}{11}, 3z+4, \\frac{2}{3}u+1, \\text { etc. }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , are all linear polynomials. Polynomials such as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x + 5 - x^{2}, x^{3} + 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , etc., are not linear polynomials."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.717,
            0.901,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A polynomial of degree 2 is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "quadratic polynomial",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The name ‘quadratic has been derived from the word ‘quadrate’, which means ‘square’. "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 x ^ { 2 } + 3 x - { \\frac { 2 } { 5 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y^{2}-2,\\quad 2-x^{2}+\\sqrt{3}x,\\quad \\frac{u}{3}-2u^{2}+5,\\quad \\sqrt{5}v^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}v,\\quad 4z^{2}+\\frac{1}{7}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are some examples of quadratic polynomials (whose coefficients are real numbers). More generally, any quadratic polynomial in x is of the form "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a x ^ { 2 } + b x + c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a , b , c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are real numbers and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a   \\neq   0 .   \\mathrm { A }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " polynomial of degree 3 is called a cubic polynomial. Some examples of"
            }
          ]
        },
